        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "After trying to replace `priority` with fully configurable `extmark_opts`, there are at least two problems with this approach:\r\n- It is not quite \"plug and play\" in [the current code](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/e9a0716013abb8d2d6f8d77331600fb99eb15d51/lua/mini/hipatterns.lua#L775), as it would need `vim.deepcopy()` to be fully operational. Having one copy per highlighter application is not good but tolerable. However, allowing this `extmark_opts` to supply `end_row`, `end_col`, and `hl_group` would mean  to copy it on every pattern match, which is too much.\r\n- Playing around with implementation prototype, I found that in order to be useful, `extmark_opts` computation would need to know at least the matched highlight group, which is known only after computation for every match. Which would mean `extmark_opts` would need to be table or function (similar to `group`) and be called on every match. This is, again, a performance issue.\r\n\r\nI was hoping that simply allowing `extmark_opts` would also solve #340, but it doesn't seem like it. Right now I am leaning towards adding certain kind of extmark behaviors on case by case basis: like inline text or sign. @cnrrobertson, what other use cases did you have in mind when opening this?",
